# package.use for the INSTALLER profile only (plan/16, plan/19 §7.3).
#
# Files named profile.<name> in this directory are copied into the config root by stage 20 only
# when that profile is the one being built; every other file here is shared by all of them. USE
# is resolved once per package, so a flag that one image needs and another must not have has
# nowhere else to live.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# dev-qt/qttools[linguist] — required to BUILD the installer page, not to run it.
#
# <id>-calamares-managed (config/portage/overlay) is a Calamares view module built out of tree
# against the installed Calamares, because Calamares accepts only C++ QtPlugin view modules.
# Building out of tree means find_package(Calamares), and the exported CalamaresConfig.cmake
# opens with:
#
#     set(qt_required Core Widgets LinguistTools)
#     find_package(${qtname} CONFIG REQUIRED ${qt_required})
#
# LinguistTools is hardcoded into the REQUIRED component list every consumer of the Calamares
# CMake package inherits, whether or not that consumer has a single translatable string — and
# this one does not. The cmake package /usr/lib64/cmake/Qt6LinguistTools is gated on this USE
# flag, so with it off the module fails at configure time with
#
#     Failed to find required Qt component "LinguistTools"
#
# and the installer page cannot be built at all. Confirmed by configuring the module against the
# real installed 3.4.2, not inferred from the ebuild.
#
# The flag costs no packages anywhere: qttools' linguist? RDEPEND is a lone
# "widgets? ( !dev-qt/linguist:5 )" slot blocker, so no lock gains an atom — only qttools' own
# contents grow, and only on this profile.
#
# It does also build the Qt Linguist GUI, which has no audience on an installer medium either.
# USE has no move that keeps Qt6LinguistTools for the build while dropping the app from the
# image, so the app is deleted after the merge instead: stage 50 section 3h, which asserts it
# stays gone. That is the same treatment, for the same reason, that section 3g gives the Qt
# D-Bus Viewer — see the long note in package.use/image about qdbus and widgets.
dev-qt/qttools                    linguist

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# kde-apps/thumbnailers[-pdf] — Ghostscript, and 87 MiB of fonts nothing else in the image uses.
#
# The chain, read off the pinned tree rather than guessed (plan/20 §4.2):
#
#   kde-apps/dolphin
#     -> kde-apps/thumbnailers[pdf]
#          -> media-gfx/kio-ps-thumbnailer      RDEPEND: app-text/ghostscript-gpl, app-text/dvipsk
#               -> app-text/ghostscript-gpl     RDEPEND: >=media-fonts/urw-fonts-2.4.9   (18 MiB)
#                                                        app-text/poppler-data
#                                        l10n_zh-CN? / l10n_zh-TW? ( media-fonts/arphicfonts )
#                                                                                        (69 MiB)
#               -> app-text/dvipsk -> dev-libs/kpathsea
#
# Six packages, 96.0 MiB installed and 70.9 MiB of EROFS measured on the real 0.3.0 medium, and
# all of it serves ONE Dolphin thumbnailer: /usr/lib64/qt6/plugins/kf6/thumbcreator/gsthumbnail.so
# draws the little preview for PDF and PostScript files. The arphicfonts half is not even about
# rendering PDFs — it is ghostscript's l10n_zh-CN/l10n_zh-TW RDEPEND, which is on because
# LOCALES_KEEP includes those two.
#
# app-text/poppler-data (12.3 MiB) DOES go with it, and the first reading of this said the
# opposite. app-text/poppler names the atom too, so a grep for it finds two consumers — but
# poppler's is `cjk? ( app-text/poppler-data )` and cjk is not in its default IUSE, so this image
# never had that half. Ghostscript's is unconditional. Reading a dependency without reading the
# condition it sits under is how the atom looked load-bearing; the resolver settled it.
#
# Nothing is lost by it either, for the same reason: poppler is built -cjk and has never used the
# encoding tables poppler-data ships.
#
# The resolver found nine more orphans that no reading of the ebuilds would have produced by
# hand — jbig2dec and libidn behind ghostscript, poppler-data as above, and dvipsk's kpathsea.
# See the note in plan/20 §2.7 about why the lock for a removal has to be REGENERATED rather
# than edited.
#
# INSTALLER ONLY. On the product this is plan/10 finding 6 and a genuine judgement call about
# whether PDF thumbnails in a file manager are worth 137 MiB. On a medium that boots once to run
# Calamares and is thrown away it is not a judgement call: nobody browses a PDF library on it.
# The desktop profile is untouched and still has the thumbnailer.
#
# NB this REMOVES packages rather than reshaping one, so it moves installer.lock and
# expected-packages.installer.txt — unlike the qttools flag above, which costs no atoms.
kde-apps/thumbnailers             -pdf
